From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Remora <remora@localhost>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 22:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 27/27] venus: rebuild a dead completion fence, cap publish
 staleness at 2s

The completion fence and its queue were latched at first use. The day
SurfaceFlinger lost its VkDevice and recreated it (a VRAM-ceiling crash
storm), every arm against the dead queue failed, fence_obj stayed non-NULL
so the loud no-fence fallback never engaged, and the publish path went
silent FOR HOURS while frame resolution stayed green — the encoder
keepalived its last real buffer, long freed and reading black, and every
host-side health check certified the black stream (bd remora-pobz).

Three defenses, layered:

1. Proof-of-death detection: a run of consecutive arm failures (~1 s at
   display rate), a hard WaitForFences error, or an endless-timeout fence
   (10 rounds of 1 s) all mark the fence dead. The wait thread never
   rearms on error — arming on a dead device fails forever, and the old
   rearm spun that failure silently.

2. Rebuild on the compositor's current queue: the stage path has the live
   queue in hand every frame; a dead fence is torn down there and the
   existing creation path remakes it. The old fence is leaked, not
   destroyed — its device may already be freed. If even creation fails,
   the pre-existing loud submit-time fallback finally becomes reachable.

3. A 2 s staleness cap, independent of WHY publishing stopped: the
   reconnect thread re-ships the current display frame whenever the
   encoder has received nothing for 2 s while connected. A static screen
   pays one no-op frame per 2 s; any future publish-path wedge is bounded
   at 2 s of stale picture instead of forever-black.

Wedge counters ride the existing [remora-fence]/[remora-publish] lines so
the next incident names itself instead of costing a forensic session.
---
diff --git a/src/venus/vkr_remora_track.c b/src/venus/vkr_remora_track.c
index 9dd8739..b63e9f3 100644
--- a/src/venus/vkr_remora_track.c
+++ b/src/venus/vkr_remora_track.c
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@
 #include <errno.h>
 #include <sys/socket.h>
 #include <sys/un.h>
+#include <time.h>
 
 #include <inttypes.h>
 #include <stdio.h>
@@ -301,6 +302,30 @@ static uint64_t pub_sent, pub_dropped;
 static char pub_req_buf[8 * 16];
 static size_t pub_req_have;
 
+/* The wedge counters (bd remora-pobz): arming or waiting can start failing
+ * PERMANENTLY — the day SurfaceFlinger lost its VkDevice and recreated it, the
+ * fence stayed latched to the dead queue, every arm failed, and the publish
+ * path went silent for hours while frame resolution stayed green. Counted and
+ * printed so the next wedge names itself instead of costing a forensic
+ * session; arm_fail_row is the consecutive run that triggers a rebuild. */
+static uint64_t n_fence_arm_fail, n_fence_wait_err, arm_fail_row, n_pub_refresh;
+static uint64_t last_publish_us; /* any successful publish, any path */
+/* Consecutive arm failures before the fence is torn down and rebuilt on the
+ * compositor's current queue. At 60 fps that is one second of proof; a single
+ * hard wait error skips the count entirely. */
+#define FENCE_REBUILD_ROW 60
+/* Consecutive 1 s wait timeouts before an unsignallable fence is declared
+ * dead — it holds fence_busy, and the rebuild is gated on that clearing. */
+#define FENCE_TIMEOUT_ROW 10
+
+static uint64_t
+now_us(void)
+{
+   struct timespec ts;
+   clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts);
+   return (uint64_t)ts.tv_sec * 1000000 + (uint64_t)ts.tv_nsec / 1000;
+}
+
 /* VkFormat -> DRM fourcc, for the formats a compositor actually presents in.
  * The byte orders are reversed between the two conventions: Vulkan names
  * components in memory order, DRM names them in little-endian packed order, so
@@ -398,6 +423,7 @@ publish_frame_locked(uint32_t res_id, int fd, uint32_t w, uint32_t h)
    const ssize_t n = sendmsg(pub_client, &mh, MSG_DONTWAIT | MSG_NOSIGNAL);
    if (n == (ssize_t)sizeof(msg)) {
       pub_sent++;
+      last_publish_us = now_us();
       if (need_fd)
          map_put(&map_res_sent, res_id, 1);
    } else if (n < 0 && (errno == EAGAIN || errno == EWOULDBLOCK)) {
@@ -489,8 +515,14 @@ fence_arm_locked(void)
    mtx_unlock(&fence_queue->vk_mutex);
    if (r != VK_SUCCESS) {
       fence_frame.valid = false;
+      arm_fail_row++;
+      if (++n_fence_arm_fail == 1)
+         fprintf(stderr, "[remora-fence] arming failed (vk %d) — a dead compositor device? "
+                         "the fence rebuilds on its current queue after %d in a row\n",
+                 (int)r, FENCE_REBUILD_ROW);
       return false;
    }
+   arm_fail_row = 0;
    fence_busy = true;
    return true;
 }
@@ -499,6 +531,7 @@ static int
 fence_thread(void *unused)
 {
    (void)unused;
+   int timeout_row = 0;
    for (;;) {
       mtx_lock(&track_mutex);
       while (!fence_busy)
@@ -516,8 +549,35 @@ fence_thread(void *unused)
       n_fence_wait++;
       if (r == VK_TIMEOUT) {
          /* Keep waiting rather than publishing: an unsignalled fence means the
-          * GPU has not finished, which is precisely what must not be encoded. */
+          * GPU has not finished, which is precisely what must not be encoded.
+          * But not forever — a fence that cannot signal (a wedged device that
+          * still answers TIMEOUT) would hold fence_busy true for good, and the
+          * stage path's rebuild is gated on !fence_busy. Ten rounds is ten
+          * seconds: a GPU genuinely that far behind has no mirror to protect. */
          n_fence_timeout++;
+         if (++timeout_row >= FENCE_TIMEOUT_ROW) {
+            fprintf(stderr, "[remora-fence] fence unsignalled for %d s — declaring it dead "
+                            "so the stage path can rebuild\n", FENCE_TIMEOUT_ROW);
+            fence_frame.valid = false;
+            fence_busy = false;
+            arm_fail_row = FENCE_REBUILD_ROW;
+            timeout_row = 0;
+         }
+         mtx_unlock(&track_mutex);
+         continue;
+      }
+      timeout_row = 0;
+      if (r != VK_SUCCESS) {
+         /* A hard wait error (device lost, most likely). Do NOT rearm here:
+          * arming on a dead device fails forever, and the old code's rearm
+          * spun that failure silently. Clear the wait and let the stage path
+          * rebuild the fence on the compositor's current queue. */
+         if (++n_fence_wait_err == 1)
+            fprintf(stderr, "[remora-fence] WaitForFences failed (vk %d) — device lost? "
+                            "the stage path rebuilds the fence\n", (int)r);
+         fence_frame.valid = false;
+         fence_busy = false;
+         arm_fail_row = FENCE_REBUILD_ROW; /* one wait error is proof enough */
          mtx_unlock(&track_mutex);
          continue;
       }
@@ -560,6 +620,26 @@ reconnect_thread(void *unused)
          publish_connect_locked();
          if (pub_client >= 0)
             publish_frame_locked(disp_res_id, disp_fd, disp_w, disp_h);
+      } else if (pub_client >= 0 && disp_res_id
+                 && now_us() - last_publish_us > 2000000) {
+         /* CONNECTED, but nothing has reached the encoder for 2 s. On a live
+          * screen that means the completion path is wedged (the night this was
+          * written: a fence latched to a dead device); on a static screen it
+          * is one no-op frame every 2 s. Either way, re-shipping the CURRENT
+          * display frame bounds ANY publish-path failure at ~2 s of staleness
+          * — the alternative, lived once, was an encoder keepaliving a freed
+          * black buffer for hours while every health check stayed green
+          * (bd remora-pobz). Same mid-render trade the reconnect one-shot
+          * above already makes, at 0.5 Hz worst case. */
+         publish_frame_locked(disp_res_id, disp_fd, disp_w, disp_h);
+         n_pub_refresh++;
+         if (n_pub_refresh == 1 || (n_pub_refresh % 300) == 0)
+            fprintf(stderr,
+                    "[remora-publish] refresh publish #%" PRIu64 " (fence pub=%" PRIu64
+                    " arm_fail=%" PRIu64 " wait_err=%" PRIu64 ") — %s\n",
+                    n_pub_refresh, n_fence_publish, n_fence_arm_fail, n_fence_wait_err,
+                    n_fence_publish ? "the screen is static or the completion path stalled"
+                                    : "the completion path has never published");
       }
       mtx_unlock(&track_mutex);
    }
@@ -575,6 +655,33 @@ fence_stage_locked(struct vkr_queue *queue, uint32_t res_id, uint32_t w, uint32_
    struct vkr_device *dev = queue->device;
    struct vn_device_proc_table *vk = &dev->proc_table;
 
+   /* REBUILD A FENCE THAT STOPPED WORKING (bd remora-pobz). The fence and its
+    * queue were latched at first use, which wedged the whole publish path the
+    * day SurfaceFlinger lost its VkDevice and recreated it: every arm against
+    * the dead queue failed, fence_obj stayed non-NULL so the loud no-fence
+    * fallback never engaged, and the stream sat silently stale for hours
+    * while frame resolution stayed green. Proof of death is a RUN of arm
+    * failures (~1 s at display rate; a hard wait error skips the count), and
+    * the compositor's CURRENT queue is in hand right here — rebuild on it.
+    * Deliberately NOT keyed on the queue pointer alone: pointers are reused
+    * by allocation and legitimately vary on multi-queue compositors, and the
+    * failure run already catches every dead queue within a second. The old
+    * fence is LEAKED, not destroyed: its device may already be freed, and
+    * vkDestroyFence into a dead device trades a one-fence leak for a crash.
+    * Gated on !fence_busy so state is never pulled out from under the wait
+    * thread; the wait thread guarantees fence_busy clears — errors clear it
+    * directly, and an endless-timeout fence is declared dead after
+    * FENCE_TIMEOUT_ROW rounds. */
+   if (fence_obj != VK_NULL_HANDLE && !fence_busy && arm_fail_row >= FENCE_REBUILD_ROW) {
+      fprintf(stderr, "[remora-fence] %" PRIu64 " consecutive arm failures — rebuilding the "
+                      "completion fence on the compositor's current queue\n", arm_fail_row);
+      fence_obj = VK_NULL_HANDLE;
+      fence_queue = NULL;
+      fence_frame.valid = false;
+      fence_next.valid = false;
+      arm_fail_row = 0;
+   }
+
    if (fence_obj == VK_NULL_HANDLE) {
       const VkFenceCreateInfo ci = { .sType = VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_FENCE_CREATE_INFO };
       if (vk->CreateFence(dev->base.handle.device, &ci, NULL, &fence_obj) != VK_SUCCESS) {
